implementationPlan.description:
TESTS FIRST (red). Load test-driven-development.

Prerequisite: task-6fc0096b is DONE and landed (c8a2a173 on moe/work-2026-10-24). Edit its landed bytes. Re-read first:
- MoeMessageDispatcher.kt, the STATE_SNAPSHOT branch;
- MoeProjectService.kt: the onProjectMismatch lambda, openClient, and onMessage's retired-client check;
- MoeDaemonSupervisor.kt: the companion's isSameProject, and deleteDaemonInfoIfUnchanged;
- the fixtures in MoeProjectForeignDaemonTest.kt, MoeProjectConnectionTest.kt and MoeMessageDispatcherAccountsTest.kt.
If a landed name differs from this plan, use the landed one. Never add a second path normalizer or deleter.

Draft the file in /mnt/d/moe-scratch/task-007c0ea8/. Copy it into the tree only once you hold the jetbrains-gradle lease (step 4.1), and keep the lease until step 3 is green, so no peer's gate compiles your red file (mem:gotcha-jetbrains-shared-tree-gate-peer-red).

New file moe-jetbrains/src/test/kotlin/com/moe/services/MoeSnapshotProjectCheckTest.kt, JUnit 4. The paths below are values, not Kotlin literals.

A. Dispatcher rows. Build MoeMessageDispatcher as MoeMessageDispatcherAccountsTest does (invokeLater = { it() }). Use a Proxy fake Project whose getBasePath returns the row's base, an onProjectMismatch that counts its calls, and an updateState that records the state. Each row sends one STATE_SNAPSHOT frame whose payload.project has id, name and rootPath, with the path Gson-encoded.
- S1, this project in any spelling. A literal table. Every row is equal under comparableProjectPath, so the new code touches no file system or network on any host. The names cannot exist on a real disk, because today's code (the red run) canonicalizes and deletes under them.
  - Base D:/moe-snapshot-proj (IntelliJ's own Windows spelling): D:\moe-snapshot-proj, D:/moe-snapshot-proj, d:\MOE-SNAPSHOT-PROJ\, D:/moe-snapshot-proj/sub/.., /mnt/d/moe-snapshot-proj, /mnt/d/moe-snapshot-proj/, //wsl$/Ubuntu/mnt/d/moe-snapshot-proj, \\wsl.localhost\Ubuntu\mnt\d\moe-snapshot-proj.
  - Base //wsl$/Ubuntu/home/moe-test/proj (a project opened through the WSL share): /home/moe-test/proj, \\wsl$\Ubuntu\home\moe-test\proj, //wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/moe-test/proj/.
  - Base /home/moe-test/proj: //wsl$/Ubuntu/home/moe-test/proj.
  Each row, with a message naming it: zero mismatch calls, and the recorded state's project.rootPath equals the row's rootPath.
- S2, another project. Real temp folders: A is the base, with a .moe/daemon.json written; B is the other project. The rows are B's absolutePath, and B in its other-side spelling from a local helper like MoeProjectForeignDaemonTest.wslSpelling (D:\x gives /mnt/d/x, and /x gives //wsl$/Ubuntu/x). Each row: exactly one mismatch call, updateState never called, and A's daemon.json still exists byte for byte, because the dispatcher never deletes. These rows reach the canonicalPath fallback, so they use real folders, never a literal drive the host may lack.

B. Service rows over a loopback WebSocket server. The fixture has:
- a temp project dir P with .moe/;
- MoeProjectService(fakeProject(P.absolutePath)) { it() };
- its daemonSupervisor field replaced by reflection with MoeDaemonSupervisor(basePath, "Test", processStarter = { error("must not spawn") }), as MoeProjectForeignDaemonTest's connect-and-retry row does, so a reconnect that fires before dispose never starts a real daemon;
- an org.java_websocket WebSocketServer on 127.0.0.1:0, like MoeProjectConnectionTest's, whose onOpen sends the row's STATE_SNAPSHOT;
- daemon.json written as DaemonInfo(server.port, 4242, "S1", basePath) through Gson;
- the client opened through the private openClient(DaemonInfo) by reflection, so no /health probe runs (MoeProjectForeignDaemonTest owns that path).
Every wait is a bounded poll (5 s) with a failure message: getState() != null for C1, and the reconnectFuture field != null for C2 and C3. close() disposes the service, closes the clients, stops the server and deletes the temp dirs. Clear MoeProjectRegistry ref counts before and after.
- C1, this project in its other-side spelling. Derive it from the EXACT basePath string given to the fake project, never from canonicalPath: Windows TEMP may hold 8.3 names (mem:gotcha-jetbrains-read-daemon-info-deletes). Expect:
  - getState()!!.project.rootPath equals that spelling;
  - isConnected(), and the wsClient field is non-null;
  - reconnectFuture is null;
  - the daemon.json bytes are unchanged.
- C2, another project (a second temp dir). Expect: reconnectFuture non-null; not connected; wsClient null; getState() null, because the foreign state never applies; daemon.json removed.
- C3, daemon.json changed in one field since connect. One fresh fixture for each of pid, port, startedAt and projectPath (the other temp dir's path): write the read info, overwrite daemon.json with the one-field replacement BEFORE opening the client, then open the client with the read info. Expect: it still disconnects and schedules the reconnect, and daemon.json equals the replacement text byte for byte.

Red proof, under the lease: run step 4's gate command with --tests com.moe.services.MoeSnapshotProjectCheckTest. On today's code expect red from S1's /mnt and WSL-share rows, S2 (today's dispatcher deletes A's daemon.json), C1 and C3. C2 may pass: it pins today's reconnect. Record which rows failed and why. A compile error is not red proof.

Error handling in the tests: no row starts or signals a real process, every wait is bounded, and every server, client, service and temp dir is closed in finally. Kotlin note: write arrayOf<Any>(...) for mixed arrays (mem:gotcha-jetbrains-gate-snapshot).

